import sys
from pathlib import Path
TD=Path(__file__).resolve().parent;sys.path.insert(0,str(TD.parent))
from manuscript_support import build
E=[
('01-00','Women’s productive and social roles','Women’s labour in rural societies has included food production, farming, processing, household provision and community activity.','Roles vary across time, place and households. Unpaid work contributes to well-being even when it is absent from wage records; a role description is not a justification for unequal opportunity.'),
('01-01','Labour','Women contribute physical and mental labour in paid and unpaid activities.','Counting wages alone can conceal food preparation, care or productive household work, so assess tasks and time rather than assuming no earnings means no contribution.'),
('01-02','Food production','Women can contribute through planting, tending, harvesting, processing and distributing food.','Access to land, tools, time and decision-making affects this contribution. Work performed does not establish control over its product or income.'),
('01-03','Family welfare','Care, food preparation, water collection and household organisation support family welfare.','Unequal caring burdens can restrict time for learning or paid work. Sharing responsibilities or improving services can expand choices without devaluing care.'),
('02-00','Culture and position','Cultural values and practices influence expectations about work, property and participation.','They can support cooperation or restrict opportunities depending on their form and use; culture is diverse and changing, not a fixed explanation of all women’s experiences.'),
('02-01','Values','Values concern what people regard as desirable or acceptable.','A value placed on mutual support may encourage shared work, while an expectation that leadership belongs to one gender can restrict voice. Explain the mechanism rather than stereotyping a community.'),
('02-02','Customs','Customs and practices shape how social decisions are carried out.','A meeting timetable or speaking arrangement can affect who participates. Consultation can reveal whether a practice supports or restricts access and what affected people want changed.'),
('02-03','Taboos and myths','A taboo restricts an activity or discussion; a myth may be used to explain or justify a belief.','If a belief excludes women from a productive role regardless of skill, it narrows opportunity. Describing that effect does not endorse the belief or claim it is shared by all members.'),
('03-00','Assess colonial changes','Colonial arrangements could reshape education, land access, wages, migration and commercial production, with differing effects on women.','Evaluate access, workload and control separately. New wage activity can coexist with exclusion from land or training; one opportunity is not proof of overall equality.'),
('03-01','Education access','Exclusion from schooling or training can restrict access to skills and recognised opportunities.','Where selection favours men despite comparable ability, women’s productive choices can be narrowed; a later formal admission rule does not automatically remove fees, distance or time barriers.'),
('03-02','Land entitlement','Access to and control over productive land influence what a person can grow and decide.','Where women work land but lack secure decision rights, their labour contribution need not give control over investment or returns. Distinguish labour performed from entitlement.'),
('03-03','Wages and labour','Paid employment can provide income while discriminatory selection, pay or conditions limit its gains.','Compare access, earnings and workload together. Entering wage work does not remove unpaid responsibilities unless their arrangement changes.'),
('03-04','Migratory labour','Labour migration separates work locations and can change household income and task allocation.','Remittances may help, but absent workers can leave others with more farming and care. The balance depends on actual transfers, timing and available support.'),
('03-05','Commercial farming','Commercial expansion can alter land access, labour demand and what households produce for sale.','Women may gain earnings while facing reduced food-growing access or control of proceeds. Assess whose opportunities and resources change rather than assuming a common benefit.')]
L={s:(m,e) for s,h,m,e in E};Q={}
def q(n,p,a,g):Q[n]=(p,a,g)
q(1,'Identify one productive contribution women can make outside recorded wage employment.','Growing household food or processing produce.',['Feature: a concrete unpaid productive contribution.'])
q(2,'Explain why wage records can understate women’s contribution to family welfare.','They can omit unpaid care, food and household production that support well-being. No recorded wage therefore does not establish no work or contribution.',['Reason: connect the measurement boundary to omitted useful activity.'])
for n,s in [(3,'01-01'),(4,'01-02'),(5,'01-03')]:q(n,'Describe two aspects of '+dict((x[0],x[1]) for x in E)[s].lower()+'.',' '.join(L[s]),['Feature: '+L[s][0],'Feature: '+L[s][1]])
for n,s in [(6,'02-00'),(8,'02-01'),(9,'02-02'),(10,'02-03')]:q(n,'Explain how '+dict((x[0],x[1]) for x in E)[s].lower()+' can influence women’s opportunities.',' '.join(L[s]),['Reason: develop a link from an expectation or practice to access or voice, without treating all women’s experiences as identical.'])
q(7,'Explain the participation effect in a fictional village where meetings occur during women’s allocated water-collection time and decisions proceed without their input.','The timetable makes attendance conflict with an assigned task, so those affected lose an opportunity to influence decisions. Changing time or sharing the collection task could reduce that particular barrier.',['Mechanism: time conflict linked to missing input.','Consequence: restricted influence; no assumption of lack of interest.'])
q(11,'Explain whether a fictional historical change establishes an overall improvement for women: wage work becomes available, but women lose food-growing access and retain the same unpaid workload. Judge livelihood choice and welfare.','The wage opportunity may provide income, but reduced food access and unchanged unpaid burdens can offset it. An overall verdict needs earnings, food costs and workload evidence; wage availability alone does not establish improved choice or welfare.',['Judgement: weigh gains and losses against livelihood choice and welfare.','Reason: use all supplied changes and identify what evidence is missing.'])
q(12,'Describe this fictional historical comparison: before a change, women hold 60% of household food plots and 10% of paid jobs; afterwards, 30% of plots and 25% of paid jobs.','Paid-job participation rises 15 percentage points while plot control falls 30 points. The two access measures move in opposing directions, so more paid work does not establish wider access to all productive resources.',['Evidence: accurate paired changes.','Relationship: contrast paid opportunity and plot control.'])
cases={13:('comparable-skilled women are excluded from training while new jobs require its certificate','Exclusion prevents access to the recognised skill route and narrows eligibility for the new work.'),14:('women cultivate land but another body can remove their access before harvest','Insecure access threatens the ability to benefit from cultivation and can discourage improvements despite their work.'),15:('paid work becomes available but caring duties remain unchanged and working hours overlap','The new earnings opportunity competes with existing unpaid tasks; workload or access barriers persist unless responsibilities or timing change.'),16:('workers migrate, send little income home and leave planting tasks without replacements','Low transfers do not compensate for lost labour, so remaining household members face additional work and possible planting delays.'),17:('commercial crops expand onto household food plots while women cannot decide how sales income is spent','The household becomes more dependent on purchased food while women lack control over the replacement income, potentially narrowing their practical choices.')}
for n,(case,a) in cases.items():q(n,'Explain the opportunity or workload mechanism in this fictional historical case: '+case+'.',a,['Mechanism: use both supplied conditions.','Consequence: '+a])
P={'P01':(['01-00','03-00'],'State (a) two useful measures of women’s contribution beyond wages [2]. Explain (b) why a fictional change bringing paid jobs but removing food-growing access cannot be judged from job numbers alone [4].','(a) Food production and time spent providing care. (b) Paid jobs may add income while lost plots remove food and productive control; earnings and replacement food costs are needed to weigh the combined livelihood effect.',['Point: two relevant non-wage measures [2].','Development: connect the opposing supplied changes to a bounded welfare judgement [4].']),
'P02':(['02-00','03-00'],'Describe (a) this fictional comparison: A offers training at 40% participation and meetings at 20%; B offers 70% and 60% [3]. Suggest (b) a priority for A if both timetable clashes and lack of care cover are reported and the aim is fair access [3].','(a) B combines higher training and meeting participation, by 30 and 40 percentage points. (b) Arrange workable times and care support with affected participants, then monitor access; those changes address stated barriers rather than interpreting low participation as unwillingness.',['Evidence: paired participation comparisons [3].','Meaning: action matched to both barriers and the fair-access aim [3].'])}
examples=[(n,'Read the practical connection',Q[k][0]+' '+Q[k][1],[s for s in L if s.startswith(n+'-')]) for n,k in [('01',2),('02',7),('03',11)]]
links={n:'Connect to Rural Development (5.1) and modern roles (6.2). Separate actual work, access to resources and decision-making influence. Historical mechanisms here are illustrative; no uniform experience or current legal rule is assumed.' for n in ['01','02','03']}
if __name__=='__main__':build(TD,E,Q,P,links,examples)
